Because hit points are so low at XP=1 it is very easy to die even
with running away whenever damage is taken.  Having a fresh
batch of monsters from the initial population of level 2 makes it
that much riskier to explore at XP=1.

The equivalent question becomes: Why would you want to
descend before being prepared, knowing that monster generation
includes the level so that going deeper means meaner monsters
whether you level up or not?

As to loot, in the early game that's a matter of going into the
mines not exploring a level at a time.

It disagrees with the strategy of avoiding leveling up, but it is
not wrong simply for that reason.  Consider that serial ascenders
don't often play the protection racket.  Avoiding leveling up is a
riskier strategy than not avoiding leveling up.  Then again
pursuing leveling up to the point you're competing with your pet
is also riskier as well.  The best odds are somewhere in the
center.

Standard strategy - As soon as I discover the stairs step down,
look at that one room, step back up.  The level is populated with
monsters based on the XP and maximum depth at that time not
when I do descend to explore fully.



